# PART 1 — GLOBAL DESIGN SYSTEM, FONTS, ADVANCED SAAS UI & PROFESSIONAL ANIMATIONS

**Priority:** First  
**Target:** Establish a consistent, premium design foundation without breaking the existing application shell or changing existing property imagery.

## 1.1 Final brand direction

The following palette is the approved HOMIO visual direction for this specification. Use it consistently across the frontend after auditing the existing implementation.

| Token | Colour | Intended use |
|---|---|---|
| Deep Espresso | `#241C1A` | Primary dark surfaces, navigation, headings and selected dark sections |
| Dark Rosewood | `#75464A` | Restrained brand accent, selected states and carefully chosen emphasis |
| Matte Antique Gold | `#A98556` | Premium highlights, fine borders, selected indicators and restrained CTAs |
| Matte Ivory | `#F7F4EF` | Main page background and spacious light surfaces |
| Warm Stone | `#EAE1D7` | Secondary surfaces, subtle dividers and muted panels |
| Pure White | `#FFFFFF` | Cards, forms, dialogs and clean content surfaces |

### Brand and appearance rules

- The overall look must be rich, dark, matte, elegant and international.
- Deep Espresso is the primary dark colour. Dark Rosewood is a restrained signature accent, not a bright or dominant pink.
- Matte Antique Gold must remain restrained. Do not make entire pages or large surfaces gold.
- Matte Ivory and Warm Stone should provide warmth and visual breathing room.
- Pure White is reserved for clean, readable cards, forms and panels.
- Do not use neon colours, glossy buttons, shiny metallic gradients, exaggerated glow, excessive shadows or bright pink accents.
- Do not apply the palette mechanically to every element. Preserve readable contrast, visual hierarchy and meaningful status colours.
- Use accessible contrast for text, controls, disabled states, focus indicators and interactive elements. If a colour combination fails contrast requirements, adjust the foreground/background pairing rather than sacrificing accessibility.
- Use semantic design tokens rather than scattering repeated literal colour values across components.
- Preserve existing information hierarchy and usable layouts while aligning their appearance with the approved brand direction.
- This is a controlled visual-system update, not permission to redesign every screen or rebuild the frontend.

Suggested semantic token names may include:

```css
--color-brand-primary: #241C1A;
--color-brand-accent: #75464A;
--color-brand-gold: #A98556;
--color-background: #F7F4EF;
--color-surface-muted: #EAE1D7;
--color-surface: #FFFFFF;
```

These names are examples of the intended token contract, not an instruction to create a second token system. Inspect the current tokens first and reuse or extend the canonical implementation.

## 1.2 Typography direction

The typography should feel premium and editorial while remaining practical for a global real-estate and SaaS product.

### Headings

- Prefer a refined serif typeface for major page headings, hero titles and selected editorial headings **only if an appropriate existing, licensed font asset or reliable approved font source is available**.
- Use restrained weights and a clear hierarchy.
- Avoid overly decorative, thin or difficult-to-read heading styles.
- Preserve legibility on small screens and long international place names.

### Body and interface

- Use a clean, modern sans-serif font stack for body copy, forms, buttons, navigation, labels, data tables and SaaS dashboards.
- Prioritize readability, consistent spacing and predictable text wrapping.
- Use tabular numerals where appropriate for prices, counts and financial values if supported by the selected typeface.
- Avoid inconsistent font weights, arbitrary font-size overrides and layout-breaking letter spacing.

### Font-loading constraints

- Audit `layout.tsx`, existing font imports, font assets, CSS tokens and build configuration before changing font loading.
- Do not invent font filenames or reference assets that do not exist.
- Do not introduce a network-dependent font-loading requirement that makes builds or production rendering unreliable.
- Prefer existing local font assets when available and properly licensed.
- If a suitable serif asset is not already available, use a verified and properly licensed option only after checking its loading method and dependency implications. Otherwise retain a sensible serif fallback stack for headings.
- Keep a sensible sans-serif fallback stack for interface and body text.
- Prevent unnecessary font-induced layout shifts. Use appropriate fallback metrics and loading behaviour where supported.
- Do not add a new font dependency merely to satisfy the visual direction if the same result can be achieved safely with the existing setup.

