## 1.5 Design-system contract

Centralize and document:

- Brand, semantic, text, surface, border, feedback and disabled-state colours.
- Font families, font weights, typography scale, line heights and heading hierarchy.
- Spacing, container widths, breakpoints and responsive layout rules.
- Border radii, shadows, overlays, stacking order and focus-ring styles.
- Motion duration, easing, distance, delay and reduced-motion behaviour.
- Standard states for hover, active, focus-visible, disabled, loading, error and success.
- Consistent form fields, buttons, cards, tabs, dialogs, drawers, sheets, toasts, skeletons and navigation surfaces.

All shared CSS modules should consume the same design tokens. Audit existing hard-coded navy, blue-grey, gold and other colour values. Replace them only when they conflict with the approved system; preserve meaningful status colours and existing visual states that carry information.

Do not create parallel global tokens or competing component styles. If existing tokens already serve a purpose, extend those tokens and migrate dependent consumers coherently.

### Property image preservation — mandatory

The approved colour, font and motion update must **not alter existing property imagery**.

Unless a separate image change is explicitly approved, preserve:

- Existing image files and asset references.
- Existing image URLs and `src`/`srcSet` values.
- Existing image selection and order in galleries, cards and property/project pages.
- Existing crop, `object-fit`, `object-position` and focal-point settings.
- Existing aspect ratios, image container dimensions and layout allocation.
- Existing gallery interactions, thumbnails and image-navigation behaviour.
- Existing fallback-image logic and loading behaviour.

Do not replace, regenerate, recolour, retouch, reorder, reposition, zoom or otherwise alter property images as part of this visual refresh.

Do not add image zoom to property-card hover animations. Restrained card border and shadow transitions are acceptable only when they do not change the image's rendered appearance or framing.

During implementation, compare image-related markup, URLs, CSS properties and layout dimensions before and after changes. If a proposed shared-style change could affect image rendering, isolate it from the image styles or leave the relevant image rule unchanged.

A property-image change is outside the scope of this Part and requires its own explicit approval.

## 1.6 Motion behaviour

| Interaction | Expected behaviour |
|---|---|
| Buttons and chips | Subtle colour, border and press transitions |
| Property cards | Restrained border, shadow or surface transition; no image zoom or image-framing change |
| Mega menus | Smooth opacity and short vertical movement |
| Modals and drawers | Controlled fade/slide without abrupt appearance |
| Selected content sections | Optional subtle reveal; no compulsory animation on every element |
| Loading states | Calm skeleton pulse without distracting shimmer |
| Navigation and tabs | Clear, short state transitions without delaying interaction |
| Reduced motion | Disable or substantially reduce nonessential motion |

Rules:

- CSS-first motion; do not add a heavy animation dependency without demonstrated need.
- No layout jumps or animation-dependent functionality.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.
- Keep keyboard focus clearly visible.
- Avoid unnecessary animation on large listing collections.
- Ensure motion styles are actually imported and applied.
- Do not add `MotionReveal` to every page indiscriminately.
- Prefer restrained transitions and short durations over theatrical animations.
- Do not use motion that delays navigation, form submission or essential feedback.
- Do not animate property images or change their crop, position, scale or visual content.
- Avoid hover-only information that cannot be accessed on touch devices or with a keyboard.
- Ensure animations do not create horizontal overflow or shift surrounding content.
- Keep focus indicators visible during and after transitions.
- Motion must degrade safely when reduced-motion preferences are enabled.

## 1.7 Integration requirements

- Inspect `layout.tsx`, `globals.css`, tokens, base styles and foundation styles before editing.
- Preserve `AppShell`, `ErrorBoundary`, `ProductionQualityMonitor`, metadata and existing global imports.
- Integrate motion CSS through the canonical global style entry point.
